.TH PAGE-TYPES 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
page-types \- summarize, list and act on the flags of physical memory pages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B page-types
.RB [ \-rlLCMNiXx ]
.RB [ \-a
.IR addr-spec ]...\&
.RB [ \-b
.IR bits-spec ]...\&
.RB [ \-c
.IR cgroup ]
.RB [ \-p
.I pid
|
.B \-f
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-F
.IR file ]
.br
.B page-types \-d
.I flags
.br
.B page-types \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B page-types
reads the flags of physical page frames from
.I \%/proc/kpageflags
and prints how many pages have each combination of flags.
It can restrict the pages it examines to ranges of page frames, to the
pages mapped by a process or to the page-cache pages of files, select
pages by their flags or memory cgroup, list the selected pages, and mark
them idle or poison and unpoison them.
.PP
By default every page frame is examined, and the page offsets used by
.B \-a
are page frame numbers (PFNs).
With
.BR \-p ,
the pages mapped in the address space of the process are examined, and
the offsets used by
.B \-a
are virtual page numbers (virtual addresses divided by the page size);
pages of the process that have been swapped out are included.
With
.BR \-f ,
the page-cache pages of the regular file
.IR path ,
or of every regular file below the directory
.IR path ,
are examined, and the offsets used by
.B \-a
are page offsets within each file.
Symbolic links are not followed and mount points are not crossed.
Only pages that are already in the page cache are examined:
.B page-types
maps each file, touches the pages that are resident, and looks up their
page frames.
.SS Output
Unless
.B \-N
is given,
.B page-types
prints a summary with one line for each combination of flags found: the
flags as a hexadecimal number, the number of pages, their size in
megabytes, the flags in short symbolic form, and the flags in long
symbolic form.
A final line gives the totals.
With
.BR \-f ,
the summary is preceded by the name, inode number, size and modification
and access times of
.IR path .
.PP
The short symbolic form has one character for each named flag, in bit
order, with an underscore for a flag that is not set.
The long symbolic form is a comma-separated list of the names of the
flags that are set.
.PP
With
.B \-l
or
.BR \-L ,
a list of pages, preceded by a header line, is printed before the
summary.
Each line contains, separated by tabs: the virtual page number (with
.BR \-p )
or file page offset (with
.BR \-f ),
the memory cgroup inode number prefixed with
.B @
(with
.BR \-C ),
the map count (with
.BR \-M ),
the page frame number, the number of pages (with
.B \-l
only), and the short symbolic flags.
Page numbers, offsets and counts of pages are printed in hexadecimal.
For a swapped-out page of a process, the swap offset is printed in place
of the page frame number.
.SS Flag processing
Without
.BR \-r ,
the flags in bits 32 to 47 are cleared, and the \%compound_head and
\%compound_tail flags are cleared unless the huge flag is set, so that they
are reported only for hugeTLB pages.
.PP
With
.BR \-r ,
all flags reported by the kernel are kept, and some flags that the
kernel overloads are decoded: an anonymous page with \%owner_2 set is
reported as \%anon_exclusive, a slab page with active set as \%slub_frozen,
a slab page with error set as \%slub_debug, and a page with reclaim set but
writeback clear as readahead.
With
.B \-p
or
.BR \-f ,
the softdirty, file, swap and \%mmap_exclusive flags are also taken from
the page table entry.
.PP
The flag names are:
.PP
.RS
.nf
locked  error  referenced  uptodate  dirty  lru  active  slab
writeback  reclaim  buddy  mmap  anonymous  swapcache  swapbacked
\%compound_head  \%compound_tail  huge  unevictable  hwpoison  nopage
ksm  thp  offline  \%zero_page  \%idle_page  pgtable
.fi
.RE
.PP
and, reported only with
.BR \-r :
.PP
.RS
.nf
reserved  mlocked  \%owner_2  private  \%private_2  \%owner_private
arch  softdirty  \%arch_2  \%anon_exclusive  readahead  \%slub_frozen
\%slub_debug  file  swap  \%mmap_exclusive
.fi
.RE
.PP
Their meanings are described in
.I \%Documentation/admin\-guide/mm/pagemap.rst
in the Linux source.
.SS Flag arguments
The
.I flags
argument of
.B \-d
and the flags in a
.I bits-spec
are comma-separated lists of flag names and numbers.
A name may be abbreviated to a prefix; the prefix is compared with the
flag names in bit order and the first match is used, so that, for
example,
.B a
means
.BR active .
Any prefix of
.BR compound ,
even a single
.BR c ,
selects both \%compound_head and \%compound_tail.
A number, in decimal, in hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
prefix or in octal with a
.B 0
prefix, is taken as a mask of flag bits.
Anything else is an error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-addr " \fIaddr-spec\fP"
Examine only the pages in the range
.IR addr-spec ,
which has one of these forms, in units of pages:
.RS
.TP 8
.I N
the page at offset
.IR N ;
.TP
.IB N + M
the
.I M
pages starting at
.IR N ;
.TP
.IB N , M
the pages from
.I N
to
.IR M \-1;
.TP
.IB N ,
the pages from
.I N
to the end;
.TP
.BI , M
the pages from 0 to
.IR M \-1.
.RE
.IP
Numbers are read as in
.IR "Flag arguments" .
The option may be given up to 1024 times; the ranges are examined in the
order given.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bits " \fIbits-spec\fP"
Select only pages whose flags match
.IR bits-spec ,
which has one of these forms:
.RS
.TP 8
.IB bit1 , bit2
at least one of the listed flags is set;
.TP
.IB bit1 , bit2 = bit1
of the flags listed before
.BR = ,
exactly those listed after it are set;
.TP
.IB bit1 ,~ bit2
the flags without
.B ~
are set and the flags with
.B ~
are clear;
.TP
.BI = bit1 , bit2
the flags are exactly the listed ones.
.RE
.IP
Any number of flags may be listed.
The option may be given up to 64 times; a page must match all of them.
The flags compared are those after the processing described in
.IR "Flag processing" ,
so flags that are reported only with
.B \-r
match only with
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-cgroup " \fIcgroup\fP"
Select only pages charged to the memory cgroup
.IR cgroup ,
given either as the path of the cgroup's directory or as
.BI @ inode
with the inode number of that directory.
Swapped-out pages are not selected.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid " \fIpid\fP"
Examine the pages mapped in the address space of process
.IR pid .
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " \fIpath\fP"
Examine the page-cache pages of the regular file
.I path
or of the regular files below the directory
.IR path .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-raw
Raw mode, intended for kernel developers; see
.IR "Flag processing" .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List the selected pages, combining runs of consecutive pages with the
same flags (and the same cgroup and map count) into one line.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-list\-each
List each selected page on its own line.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-list\-cgroup
With
.B \-l
or
.BR \-L ,
include the inode number of each page's memory cgroup.
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-list\-mapcnt
With
.B \-l
or
.BR \-L ,
include the number of times each page is mapped.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-no\-summary
Do not print the summary.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-mark\-idle
Mark each selected page idle by setting its bit in
.IR \%/sys/kernel/mm/page_idle/bitmap ,
the interface of the kernel's idle page tracking.
.TP
.BR \-X ", " \-\-hwpoison
Poison each selected page by writing its page frame number to
.I \%hwpoison/corrupt\-pfn
in debugfs.
The kernel then handles the page as if a hardware memory error had been
detected in it, which can kill the processes using it.
Without
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.B \-p
or
.BR \-f ,
this applies to every page frame in the system.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-unpoison
Unpoison each selected page by writing its page frame number to
.I \%hwpoison/unpoison\-pfn
in debugfs.
The kernel supports this only for pages poisoned by software.
.TP
.BI \-F " file"
Read the page flags from
.I file
instead of
.IR \%/proc/kpageflags .
The long form
.B \-\-kpageflags
is defined without an argument in this version, so it cannot name a
file: used alone it leaves the default in place, and
.BI \-\-kpageflags= file
is rejected as an invalid option.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-describe " \fIflags\fP"
Print
.I flags
as a hexadecimal number, in short symbolic form and in long symbolic
form, and exit.
Options that follow are ignored.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message, which includes the list of flag names, and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B page-types
exits with status 0 on success and with status 1 on error, including an
invalid option.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B \%DEBUGFS_PATH
For
.B \-X
and
.BR \-x ,
the directory where debugfs is mounted.
If it is not set,
.B page-types
uses
.I \%/sys/kernel/debug
or
.I \%/debug
if a debugfs is mounted there, then the first debugfs listed in
.IR \%/proc/mounts ,
and otherwise tries to mount debugfs on
.IR \%/sys/kernel/debug .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I \%/proc/kpageflags
The flags of each page frame, indexed by page frame number.
.TP
.I \%/proc/kpagecount
The map count of each page frame; read with
.B \-M
when listing.
.TP
.I \%/proc/kpagecgroup
The memory cgroup of each page frame; read with
.B \-c
or
.BR \-C .
.TP
.IR \%/proc/ pid \%/pagemap
.PD 0
.TP
.IR \%/proc/ pid \%/maps
.PD
The page frames and mappings of the process; read with
.BR \-p .
.TP
.I \%/proc/self/pagemap
Used with
.B \-f
to find the page frames of the mapped file pages.
.TP
.I \%/sys/kernel/mm/page_idle/bitmap
Written with
.BR \-i .
.TP
.IR debugfs \%/hwpoison/corrupt\-pfn
.PD 0
.TP
.IR debugfs \%/hwpoison/unpoison\-pfn
.PD
Written with
.B \-X
and
.BR \-x .
.SH NOTES
The kernel interfaces that
.B page\-types
uses need these kernel configuration options:
.TP
.B \%CONFIG_PROC_PAGE_MONITOR
.IR \%/proc/kpageflags ,
.I \%/proc/kpagecount
and
.IR \%/proc/ pid \%/pagemap ;
.TP
.B \%CONFIG_MEMCG
.I \%/proc/kpagecgroup
.RB ( \-c
and
.BR \-C );
.TP
.B \%CONFIG_IDLE_PAGE_TRACKING
.I \%/sys/kernel/mm/page_idle/bitmap
.RB ( \-i );
.TP
.B \%CONFIG_HWPOISON_INJECT
the hwpoison files in debugfs
.RB ( \-X
and
.BR \-x ).
.PP
Reading
.IR \%/proc/kpageflags ,
.I \%/proc/kpagecount
and
.I \%/proc/kpagecgroup
is normally restricted to privileged users.
The kernel reports page frame numbers in
.I pagemap
only to a caller with the
.B \%CAP_SYS_ADMIN
capability (on Peios,
.BR \%SeTcbPrivilege );
without it,
.B \-p
and
.B \-f
find no pages.
Poisoning and unpoisoning pages also require
.BR \%CAP_SYS_ADMIN .
.PP
With
.B \-f
and no
.B \-a
option, the range examined is the size of the first file examined, in
whole pages rounded down, and the same range is used for every file
below a directory.
A partial last page of a file is therefore not examined, and neither are
the pages of a later file beyond that range.
.PP
Idle page tracking is described in this file in the Linux source:
.RS
.I \%Documentation/admin\-guide/mm/idle_page_tracking.rst
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Summarize the flags of all pages in the system:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
page\-types
.EE
.in
.PP
List the anonymous pages of process 1234 that are not on an LRU list:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
page\-types \-p 1234 \-b anonymous,~lru \-l
.EE
.in
.PP
Show which flags the value 0x1020 stands for:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
page\-types \-d 0x1020
.EE
.in
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR proc (5)
